<?xml version='1.0' standalone='yes'?>
<!DOCTYPE PLUGIN [
<!ENTITY name      "corsairpsucenter2">
<!ENTITY author    "techanonymous">
<!ENTITY version   "2026.11.12a">
<!ENTITY launch    "CorsairPSUCenter2">
<!ENTITY gitURL    "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/techanonymous/corsair-psu-center2/main">
<!ENTITY pluginURL "&gitURL;/&name;.plg">
<!ENTITY source    "&gitURL;/archive/&name;-&version;-x86_64.txz">
<!ENTITY md5       "819fa224bc432b9189a4457b3202229d">
<!ENTITY plugdir   "/boot/config/plugins/&name;">
<!ENTITY oldname   "corsairpsucenter">
<!ENTITY olddir    "/boot/config/plugins/&oldname;">
]>

<PLUGIN name="&name;"
        author="&author;"
        version="&version;"
        pluginURL="&pluginURL;"
        launch="&launch;"
        icon="bolt"
        min="6.12.0"
        support="https://github.com/techanonymous/corsair-psu-center2/issues">

<CHANGES>
###2026.11.12a
- Fixed: restarting the plugin's collector (an update, a reinstall) no longer
  blanks the last hour of the 1h graph. Longer ranges were never affected.
###2026.11.12
- Changed: Power, Breakdown, Temperature and Fans on the Power History graph are
  now on/off layers on one chart, so you can show any combination together and
  see how power, temperatures and fan speeds move together. Watts use the left
  axis; temperature and RPM get their own scales on the right. Hover shows every
  visible value, and your layer choice is remembered.
###2026.11.11a
- New: Daily Energy bar chart - kWh per day for the last 30 or 90 days, or per
  month for the last 12 months, with cost on hover.
- New: a short description on Unraid's Plugins page.
###2026.11.11
- First release of Corsair PSU Center v2 by techanonymous (fork of racoutlaw's
  Corsair PSU Center). Installing it removes the original plugin and copies your
  settings and energy history across; the old flash folder is kept as a backup.
- New: power history graph - 1h / 6h / 12h / 24h / 7d / 30d / 365d - with
  average, peak, kWh and cost for the range, plus Temperature and Fans views.
- New: power breakdown - CPU (RAPL), GPU (nvidia-smi / amdgpu), drives
  (estimated), everything else and PSU losses, stacked to the wall draw.
- New: alerts via Unraid notifications - VRM / case temperature, load, rail
  voltage out of ATX spec, PSU fan stopped, PSU disconnected.
- New: system fans - average RPM and fan count across motherboard and fan
  controller sensors, with per-source include / exclude.
###v1 history (racoutlaw's Corsair PSU Center)
###v1 2026.11.11
- Fixed: on Unraid's dark themes (Black / Gray) the Corsair logo on the new
  Dashboard tile was hard to see - it is a black logo and blended into the dark
  background. It now shows as a white silhouette on dark themes; the light themes
  (White / Azure) keep it black. It follows a live theme change on its own. Thanks
  to PaliKinG3 for the report (issue #8).
###v1 2026.11.10
- New: a Corsair PSU tile on Unraid's main Dashboard. It shows the live power draw,
  load and efficiency, VRM and case temperatures, fan speed and mode, and today /
  this month / lifetime energy and cost at a glance - no need to open the full page.
  The arrow on the tile jumps straight to the plugin. The tile reads the same data
  the plugin already collects, so it adds no extra polling of the PSU.
###v1 2026.09.26
- The "Custom Curve" preset now applies your last saved custom curve on a single
  click, like the other presets - no separate "Apply Curve" needed to get it going.
  Your hand-drawn curve is remembered on its own, so switching to Quiet / Balanced /
  Performance and back to Custom Curve returns to your curve instead of theirs.
###v1 2026.09.25
- Fan control tidied up: every mode now lives in one Cooling Presets list -
  Default, Quiet, Balanced, Performance, Fixed and Custom Curve (the separate
  mode buttons inside the fan card are gone; that card is now just the editor).
- The Fan mode readout names the active preset (Quiet / Balanced / Performance)
  instead of always saying "Custom curve"; a hand-drawn curve still shows
  "Custom curve".
- The old "Max (100%)" button is now "Fixed" - it applies the speed you pick on
  the slider instead of forcing 100%.
- Fixed: the fixed-speed slider no longer snaps back to the stored value while
  you are dragging it, so you have time to set a duty and Apply.
###v1 2026.09.24
- Changed: removing the plugin no longer deletes your data. Uninstalling now keeps
  your settings and the full energy history (kWh + cost) on the flash, so pulling
  the plugin to troubleshoot and reinstalling picks everything right back up - only
  the downloaded package files are cleared. Delete the plugin's folder by hand if
  you want a full purge. Thanks to PaliKinG3 for the report (issue #7).
###v1 2026.09.12
- New: currency selection. Choose the currency shown for costs from the new
  Currency panel - USD, EUR, GBP, JPY and more, or a Custom symbol - and pick
  whether it sits before the amount ($1) or after it (1 EUR). Every cost updates
  to match: the tiles, Last Month, the month/year pickers and the history lists.
###v1 2026.09.10
- New: energy history. The dashboard now keeps every past month and year instead
  of losing "This Month" when it rolls over. Added a "Last Month" tile and an
  Energy History section with a month picker and a year picker (jump to any month
  or any year you have data for), 1 / 3 / 6 / 12 month and All filters, a
  month-by-month list and a by-year summary. It reads the daily data the plugin
  was already recording, so past months appear right away. History is tiny (a few
  bytes per month) and days the server was off are not counted.
- Changed: on the energy tiles the cost is now shown at full size beside the kWh
  (it was small subtext before), and the small min/max numbers on every tile were
  enlarged to match, for readability.
###v1 2026.08.20
- Fixed: a fresh install or reinstall could fail with "'/bin/bash' returned 1",
  and the plugin could vanish after a reboot. The pre-install cleanup step
  returned an error when there was no previous version to remove - which is the
  normal state on a clean system and on every fresh boot - and Unraid treated
  that as a fatal error, aborting the install before the package was registered.
  Installs now complete reliably on a clean system and the plugin persists
  across reboots. In-place upgrades were not affected.
###v1 2026.08.10
- New: energy usage + cost tracking. New dashboard tiles show power used and the
  cost for today / this week / month / year / lifetime, with a field for your
  electricity rate. A lightweight background collector integrates the PSU's
  input power into kWh; data persists on the flash across reboots and is kept
  crash-safe with atomic checkpoints (kind to the USB stick - it writes only
  every few minutes, while the on-screen number updates live from RAM).
- New: manual mains-voltage control (Auto / 115V / 230V) in the header. Some
  units (e.g. the HX1000i 2022 revision) report input voltage at about double
  the real value over their USB interface; if yours reads ~230V on a 115V outlet
  (or the reverse), pin your real mains here so the input-voltage readout and the
  efficiency / power-in estimates stay correct. Auto (the default) trusts the
  PSU, so existing setups are unchanged.
- Tidied the energy Reset tile layout.
###v1 2026.08.09
- Fan control now works on Unraid systems with no Python installed. The plugin
  bundles its own Python 3.11 runtime, so liquidctl no longer needs a system
  python3 (Unraid does not ship Python by default). Fixes "python3: command not
  found" on any fan action.
- Now fully self-contained: bundled interpreter plus every module it needs.
###v1 2026.07.26
- First release.
- iCUE-style dashboard for Corsair HXi / RMi digital power supplies.
- Live sensor tiles (per-rail volts/amps/watts, temps, fan, efficiency,
  input/output power) read from the kernel corsair-psu hwmon driver, so
  polling costs no USB traffic and cannot collide with anything else.
- Fan control: default (PSU firmware), fixed duty, or a custom curve with a
  draggable editor, plus Quiet / Balanced / Performance / Max presets.
- +12V OCP single/multi rail toggle.
- Model is auto-detected; 12 supported PSUs, each with its own efficiency
  coefficients.
- Self-contained: bundles every python module it needs. No pip, no internet
  access required at install or boot.
</CHANGES>

<!-- pre-install: remove any previously-installed version first, so an update never
     leaves the old package registered or its files orphaned. Our date-based package
     names carry no Slackware build number, so upgradepkg does not reliably match the
     old package as the same one to replace - it would install alongside it. Removing
     first guarantees a clean single install. On a fresh boot the glob matches nothing,
     so this is a harmless no-op there. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
# nullglob: on a fresh system or a fresh boot the package glob below matches
# nothing. Without this, bash iterates once over the literal unmatched pattern,
# [ -e ] returns 1, and because that is the LAST command in this script bash exits
# 1 - which Unraid treats as a fatal plugin error and aborts the whole install
# (the package is never registered, and the plugin fails to reappear after a reboot).
shopt -s nullglob
if [ -f /var/run/&name;-fand.pid ]; then
  kill "$(cat /var/run/&name;-fand.pid)" 2&gt;/dev/null
  rm -f /var/run/&name;-fand.pid
fi
if [ -f /var/run/&name;-energyd.pid ]; then
  kill "$(cat /var/run/&name;-energyd.pid)" 2&gt;/dev/null
  rm -f /var/run/&name;-energyd.pid
fi
for pkg in /var/lib/pkgtools/packages/&name;-*; do
  [ -e "$pkg" ] &amp;&amp; removepkg "$(basename "$pkg")" &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1
done
# This block is best-effort cleanup only; never let its exit status abort the install.
exit 0
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- replace v1: Corsair PSU Center v2 is a separate plugin (corsairpsucenter2), so the
     original racoutlaw plugin (corsairpsucenter) is removed here - two plugins driving the
     same PSU fan over USB would fight each other. Its settings and energy history are
     copied across the first time (only if v2 has none yet), and its folder on the flash
     is left in place untouched as a backup. -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
shopt -s nullglob
OLD=0
[ -e "/boot/config/plugins/&oldname;.plg" ] &amp;&amp; OLD=1
[ -e "/var/log/plugins/&oldname;.plg" ] &amp;&amp; OLD=1
for pkg in /var/lib/pkgtools/packages/&oldname;-[0-9]*; do OLD=1; done
if [ "$OLD" = 1 ]; then
  echo "Removing the original Corsair PSU Center (v1) - v2 replaces it."
  for p in fand energyd; do
    if [ -f "/var/run/&oldname;-$p.pid" ]; then
      kill "$(cat "/var/run/&oldname;-$p.pid")" 2&gt;/dev/null
      rm -f "/var/run/&oldname;-$p.pid"
    fi
  done
  sleep 1
  # carry settings + energy history over, once (after v1's collector has flushed on stop)
  if [ -d "&olddir;" ] &amp;&amp; [ ! -f "&plugdir;/settings.cfg" ] &amp;&amp; [ ! -d "&plugdir;/energy" ]; then
    mkdir -p "&plugdir;"
    [ -f "&olddir;/settings.cfg" ] &amp;&amp; cp -p "&olddir;/settings.cfg" "&plugdir;/settings.cfg"
    [ -d "&olddir;/energy" ] &amp;&amp; cp -rp "&olddir;/energy" "&plugdir;/energy"
    echo "  Copied your v1 settings and energy history to &plugdir;"
  fi
  # the [0-9] keeps this from ever matching our own corsairpsucenter2-* package
  for pkg in /var/lib/pkgtools/packages/&oldname;-[0-9]*; do
    removepkg "$(basename "$pkg")" &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1
  done
  rm -rf "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&oldname;"
  rm -f "/boot/config/plugins/&oldname;.plg" "/var/log/plugins/&oldname;.plg"
  rm -f "&olddir;"/*.txz
  echo "  v1 removed. Its folder &olddir; was kept as a backup."
fi
exit 0
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- download + install the package (checksum verified) -->
<FILE Name="&plugdir;/&name;-&version;-x86_64.txz" Run="upgradepkg --install-new">
  <URL>&source;</URL>
  <MD5>&md5;</MD5>
</FILE>

<!-- post-install -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash">
<INLINE>
mkdir -p &plugdir;
chmod 755 /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/fand.sh 2>/dev/null

# Warn (do not fail) if the kernel driver has not bound a supported PSU.
if ! grep -qs corsairpsu /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*/name; then
  echo ""
  echo "  NOTE: no Corsair digital PSU detected yet."
  echo "  This plugin needs a HXi/RMi series unit connected via its USB cable,"
  echo "  handled by the kernel 'corsair-psu' driver. Check the cable, then"
  echo "  reload the page."
  echo ""
fi

# If a fan curve was active before a reboot, bring the service back up.
CFG=&plugdir;/settings.cfg
if [ -f "$CFG" ] &amp;&amp; grep -q 'mode="curve"' "$CFG"; then
  setsid /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/fand.sh &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 &lt; /dev/null &amp;
fi

# Start the energy collector. It runs independently of fan mode (energy must
# accrue no matter how the fan is being driven), reads sysfs only - no USB, no
# python - and self-guards against duplicate instances.
setsid /usr/bin/php /usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/energyd.php &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1 &lt; /dev/null &amp;

echo ""
echo "-----------------------------------------------------------"
echo " Corsair PSU Center v2 &version; installed"
echo " Open the 'Corsair PSU v2' tab in the top menu."
echo "-----------------------------------------------------------"
echo ""
</INLINE>
</FILE>

<!-- uninstall -->
<FILE Run="/bin/bash" Method="remove">
<INLINE>
shopt -s nullglob
# stop the fan curve service and hand the fan back to the PSU firmware
if [ -f /var/run/&name;-fand.pid ]; then
  kill "$(cat /var/run/&name;-fand.pid)" 2&gt;/dev/null
  rm -f /var/run/&name;-fand.pid
fi
# stop the energy collector
if [ -f /var/run/&name;-energyd.pid ]; then
  kill "$(cat /var/run/&name;-energyd.pid)" 2&gt;/dev/null
  rm -f /var/run/&name;-energyd.pid
fi
HW=""
for h in /sys/class/hwmon/hwmon*; do
  [ "$(cat $h/name 2&gt;/dev/null)" = "corsairpsu" ] &amp;&amp; HW=$h
done
if [ -n "$HW" ]; then
  HID=$(grep -o 'HID_ID=[0-9A-Fa-f]*:[0-9A-Fa-f]*:[0-9A-Fa-f]*' "$HW/device/uevent" 2&gt;/dev/null)
  VID=$(echo "$HID" | cut -d: -f2); PID=$(echo "$HID" | cut -d: -f3)
  if [ -n "$VID" ]; then
    PY=/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;/py/bin/python3; [ -x "$PY" ] || PY=python3
    "$PY" -m liquidctl --vendor 0x$(printf '%04x' $((16#$VID))) \
      --product 0x$(printf '%04x' $((16#$PID))) initialize --direct-access &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1
  fi
fi

# removepkg needs the full versioned package name as it appears in
# /var/lib/pkgtools/packages - the bare plugin name does NOT match and fails
# with "No such package", silently orphaning the installed files.
# Glob so this still works after a version bump.
for pkg in /var/lib/pkgtools/packages/&name;-*; do
  [ -e "$pkg" ] &amp;&amp; removepkg "$(basename "$pkg")" &gt;/dev/null 2&gt;&amp;1
done

rm -rf "/usr/local/emhttp/plugins/&name;"
# Preserve the user's data across a remove/reinstall. Deleting &plugdir; here would
# wipe settings.cfg AND the energy history (energy/state.json + energy/daily.csv) -
# losing months of usage/cost data on an uninstall (issue #7). Instead only clear
# the downloaded package files and keep the config + history, so a reinstall picks
# them straight back up. The glob is nullglob-guarded (set at the top of this block).
for txz in "&plugdir;"/*.txz; do [ -e "$txz" ] &amp;&amp; rm -f "$txz"; done
rm -f "/boot/config/plugins/&name;.plg"
rm -f /var/run/&name;-fand.pid
echo "Corsair PSU Center v2 removed."
echo "Your settings and energy history were kept at &plugdir;"
echo "(delete that folder by hand if you want to purge everything)."
</INLINE>
</FILE>

</PLUGIN>
